  "decision": {
    "status": "boundary_is_a_mortal_wound_gradient_skip_retained",
    "adoption": "lab_only_repair_boundary_evidence",
    "reason": "Measured on the production evolve surface (regime grid x 10 seeds) and diagnosed by cause. Finding, hypothesis REFUTED: aligned-but-invalid repairs are NOT an extreme-damage-only edge \u2014 they are a damage-scaled MORTALITY GRADIENT present throughout, from 0.0 at the mildest damage to 0.0875 at the harshest, ~0.0 in the shipped session band. The cause diagnosis shows the failing invariant is the disconnected required body core \u2014 a creature whose core was shattered by repeated damage its repair could not reconnect. VERDICT: the skip is retained as correct biology. A mortality curve that scales with damage is precisely what a survival engine should produce; the shipped skip (no episode, archive_decision skipped_invalid) handles it gracefully and the game reads it as extinction pressure. A connected-core repair contract is the REJECTED alternative: forcing reconnection would flatten the mortality curve to zero, manufacturing survival the physics did not grant and breaking the first law. The refuted 'extreme-only' framing is corrected in the record; the low in-band rate keeps it a rare event, not a common frustration."
  },
  "next_research_step": "None for the queue. If gameplay later wants the mortal wound surfaced, skipped_invalid + this gradient are the source; if the in-band rate is ever judged too high for the difficulty curve, the repair model's core-preservation (not a forced contract) is the tuning lever to study."
